  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Potential for the first accumulating snowfall of\nthe season, especially across the Laurels and the northern tier\nof Pennsylvania.\n\nThe main game in town across central Pennsylvania over the next\nweek will be the potential for the first accumulating snowfall \nacross portions of central Pennsylvania. As a low pressure \nsystem approaches the region from the Mid-Ohio Valley late \nWednesday into Thursday, precipitation chances will begin to \nramp up throughout the overnight period. A slightly warmer trend\nhas been observed since this time yesterday, with virtually all\nof the GEFS ensemble members outline temperatures above the \nfreezing mark across NW PA (where temperatures will be the \ncoolest) throughout the daytime hours on Thursday, thus there \nis higher confidence on outright rain. Given that this trend \nalso extends into ENS guidance, fairly confident on there being\nno snow through sunset on Thursday.\n\nDiving into the details after sunset Thursday evening, ensemble\nguidance begins to show much more spread with respect to \ntemperatures, especially in GEFS guidance. All guidance \ncontinues to agree on a drop in 850mb temperatures from +4C to \n+5C ahead of this colder air trending closer to -4 to -5C by \nFriday morning/afternoon. This colder air will bring increased \nchances for a transition to rain/snow showers before an outright\ntransition to snow showers for areas north/west of the \nI-80/I-99 corridor by sunrise on Friday. In terms of snowfall \nforecast (only goes through Friday afternoon), have increased \nsnowfall accumulations across the highest elevations of Somerset\nCounty given NBM model guidance tends to verify closer to the \n90th percentile. This puts highest accumulations at this time \nbetween 1-2\" there while much of the rest of the area trends \nbelow the 1\" mark. Further south/east, temperatures will be \nmarginal with rain/snow mix possible in the highest elevations \nand cannot completely rule out some wet snowflakes flying Friday\nmorning however, confidence remains low in any accumulations. \nNewest WSSI guidance does outline \"Winter Weather Conditions\" \nacross portions of central Pennsylvania (mainly north/west), so \nfolks should be prepared for the potential of wintry weather for\ntheir Friday morning commute and should be prepared to drive \ncarefully given these conditions.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Northwesterly flow bring much cooler \ntemperatures this weekend, with lake-enhanced snow showers.\n\nCooler temperatures and breezy northwesterly winds will take \nhold of the region as the area of low pressure shifts east \ntowards the New England Coast. Recent medium-range guidance \nindicates the area of low pressure beginning to deepen, allowing\nfor a tightening pressure gradient across the region. This will\nmark a return of breezy conditions and much cooler than average\ntemperatures for the middle of November. At this time, \nbreeziest winds appear to be across the highest elevations along\nthe Allegheny Front with a secondary areas across southeastern \nPennsylvania. Some of the more recent guidance suggests maximum \nwind gusts exceeding 25 mph across the board on Saturday with \nthe aforementioned areas pushing closer to the 30 to 35 mph \nrange. A slight uptick in winds continues into Sunday, with \n30-35 mph winds across all of central Pennsylvania with isolated\nwind gusts upwards of 40-45 mph across the higher elevations of\nthe Laurel Highlands.\n\nThis northwesterly flow will bring a continued chance for \nprecipitation as flow across the Great Lakes becomes more \nfavorable for lake enhanced snow showers. There still remains \nsome uncertainty with respect to wind direction (will there be \ntoo much of a northerly or westerly component) so have continued\nto role with NBM model guidance in the Friday to Saturday \ntimeframe but could see some refinement in future forecast \npackages. Chances for precipitation begin to decrease on Sunday \nand into the first half of the week as synoptic setup becomes \nless favorable for lingering showers. A slight warming trend \nemerges into the beginning of next week, with temperatures \ntrending closer to climatological averages by Tuesday.",
